  "query_preview": "..High pressure centered near the Four Corners region\nthrough the early part of next week will allow monsoonal moisture\nto continue to spread into the forecast area. The latest PWAT \nanalysis shows around 1 inch across far southwest Utah, decreasing\nnorth and east to around 0.5 inches across far northeast Utah and\nsouthwest Wyoming. However, model trends show PWATs to gradually \nincrease through the day, with west-central Utah seeing PWATs \ncloser to 0.9 inches by 00z (though eastern Utah remains much \ndrier at around 0.6-0.7 inches). \n\nIsolated to scattered convection can be expected this afternoon\nand evening, mostly along/near the spine of Utah and across \nsouthwest through west-central Utah. Pockets of moderate rainfall\nwere noted across southwest Utah the past couple of days, so can\nexpect locally moderate to heavy rain to develop today across\nportions of southern Utah. Farther north, drier low levels will\nlimit precipitation rates/amounts today and allowing for more of \na wind threat across portions of central Utah.\n\nTomorrow, with PWATs closer to 0.9-1.0 inches reaching northern \nand portions of eastern Utah, more widespread and increasingly wet\nshowers and thunderstorms can be expected. Diurnal scattered to \nnumerous convection can then be expected through the coming week \nwith moisture remaining in place. With this increase, the threat \nof heavy rain and flash flooding will increase. For today, there \nis a Marginal Risk of Excessive Rainfall in southwest Utah. This \nrisk spreads north and east for tomorrow and Monday. \n\nTemperatures today will remain quite hot, with many valleys seeing\nhigh temperatures near to above 100 degrees. However, the \nincrease in moisture will aid cooler temperatures going forward.\nMany valleys are forecast to see highs only in the 80s for the\nlatter part of next week. By Thursday, the NBM only has a 32%\nchance of SLC reaching 90 degrees, and that chance only decreases\ninto next weekend.",
